Technical Troubleshooting Report: Kubernetes Pod Lifecycle and Failures
1. Fundamental Concepts: Pod Phases vs. Container States
The Pod lifecycle represents the high-level progression of a Pod's existence within a cluster. As a Senior Architect, it is vital to understand that Pods are ephemeral entities; once created, they are assigned a unique UID and remain bound to a specific node until termination. Binding occurs when the scheduler assigns a Pod to a node, a process that is irreversible for that specific UID.
A critical distinction exists between the Phase (a high-level summary in the Pod API) and the Status (the display field in kubectl output). While the phase provides a summary, the status is a user-centric field often used for troubleshooting.
Pod Phases
The status.phase field provides a simplified state machine for the Pod.
Phase
Technical Criteria
Pending
The Pod has been accepted by the API, but one or more containers are not yet ready (includes scheduling time and image pulling).
Running
The Pod is bound to a node and all containers are created. At least one container is currently running, starting, or restarting.
Succeeded
All containers in the Pod have terminated successfully (exit code 0) and will not be restarted.
Failed
All containers have terminated, and at least one container failed (non-zero exit) and is not eligible for restart.
Unknown
The Pod state cannot be retrieved, typically due to a communication failure between the kubelet and the API server.
Container States
The kubelet tracks the individual states of each container inside the Pod.
State
Meaning
Role of "Reason" Field
Waiting
The container is performing startup tasks (e.g., pulling images or applying Secret data).
Summarizes the specific cause (e.g., ImagePullBackOff).
Running
The container is executing; postStart hooks have finished.
Provides the precise timestamp of transition into this state.
Terminated
The container finished execution, either through success or a crash.
