2. Diagnosing Common Status Errors: The CrashLoopBackOff
CrashLoopBackOff is a state where a container repeatedly fails and restarts, triggering an exponential backoff to protect cluster resources.
Standard Backoff: Restarts start at 10s, doubling with each failure (20s, 40s...) and capping at 300s (5 minutes). The timer resets if the container runs successfully for 10 minutes.
v1.35 Beta Feature: KubeletCrashLoopBackOffMax: Administrators can now configure the maximum backoff delay per node using the maxContainerRestartPeriod field (range: 1s to 300s) in the kubelet configuration.
v1.33 Alpha Feature: ReduceDefaultCrashLoopBackOffDecay: When enabled, the backoff begins at a shorter 1s interval and caps at a maximum of 60s.
Primary Causes: 1. Application code bugs. 2. Configuration errors (Missing Env/ConfigMaps). 3. Resource constraints (OOMKiller). 4. Health check (Probe) failures. 5. Infrastructure dependencies (DB unavailable).
Diagnostic Checklist
Analyze Logs: Run kubectl logs <pod-name> to identify application-level stack traces or exit reasons.
Inspect Events: Use kubectl describe pod <pod-name> to view node-level events and probe failure messages.
Validate Environment: Verify that all mounted Secrets and ConfigMaps are present and correctly mapped.
Review Resource Alignment: Compare current usage against limits; OOM issues often require increasing memory requests.
Isolate Locally: Attempt to run the container image in a local environment to differentiate between code-level bugs and Kubernetes environment issues.
