7. The Pod Termination Flow
Kubernetes prioritizes graceful termination to prevent data loss or connection drops.
Graceful Termination Flow (5 Steps)
Deletion Request: API marks the Pod as "Terminating" and sets a grace period.
Pre-Stop Hook: If defined, the kubelet executes this inside the container. If it exceeds the grace period, a 2-second extension is granted before moving to the next step.
Signal Delivery: The kubelet sends a TERM signal (or custom STOPSIGNAL) to the main process.
Endpoint Removal: Simultaneously, the control plane marks the Pod as "Not Ready" in EndpointSlices to stop new traffic.
Forced Shutdown: If processes remain after the grace period, SIGKILL is sent, and the API object is removed.
Sidecars and Stop Signals
Sidecars: Sidecars (init containers with restartPolicy: Always) are terminated in reverse order of definition only after all main containers have stopped.
Custom Stop Signals (v1.33 Alpha): Users can define custom signals in the container Lifecycle. This requires the spec.os.name field to be present in the Pod spec.
Forced Termination: Triggered via kubectl delete --force --grace-period=0. This risks resource leaks as it removes the API object without waiting for node-level confirmation.
